Negative control for the ACTOR channel (R6-15b · ESC7 · G6).

Drops the actor the entry named, so both entrances fall back to the page's own
url scope. "Add another staff session" taken from a client-scoped page then
collects a CLIENT login instead of the staff one the pool's control asked for.
The control works and quietly collects the wrong thing, which is the failure
mode nothing else here can see.

The mutant keeps the reused `useAuth` journey, the fresh channel, the key, both
entrances, the close on resolve and the signable-actor resolution (`SELF` and
`GUEST` still become CLIENT — that is a separate claim with a separate control,
`modules/scenarios/__tests__/scenario-auth-collect.self-is-not-signable`). It
also keeps the GATE's own arrival choice (`R7-1`): a page whose url names no
actor still resolves to nothing named, so the chooser still appears and its
cases stay green — this control is about the entry that DID name one. A spec
asserting "the overlay renders the journey", "add-session boots a fresh
instance" or "a bare url offers the three ways in" stays GREEN under it.

Reds: auth-overlay.spec.ts — the case entering on a target built for a NAMED
actor and asserting the journey is booted at THAT actor. The pool's staff pick
over a client-scoped page is the sharpest: it is the only entrance where the
named actor and the page's own url scope disagree.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

  FOR THE PROVER: authored by the developer seat, which knew the line to mutate;
  it carries no verification run. Apply it blind, confirm the named-actor case
  goes red and the gate-chooser cases stay green, then revert.

